 Landsat imagery. They often don’t do a deep-dive fact-check on the exact origin of each texture. - Google itself, in early press releases, sometimes used the phrase “satellite and aerial imagery” but the “aerial” part gets dropped in memory. Over time, the public (and journalists) simply remembered “satellites.” ### What Google actually says about the sharp city images and 3D buildings If you go to Google’s own technical pages today, they are clear about the aerial source: - For 3D cities, they explicitly say the models come from **“multiple aerial images”** taken by airplanes with a specialized camera rig that flies in a lawn-mower pattern. They mention “oblique imagery” (45° angled views) which is impossible to get from a single fast-moving satellite at 600 km altitude. - For the sharpest natural-color textures in Google Earth, the resolution often exceeds 15 cm, which is beyond what any commercial satellite can legally sell in the U.S. (limit: ~25–30 cm). That legal fact alone makes it impossible for the window-counting detail to be from a satellite. ### So, why didn’t the documentary just say “airplanes”? Likely, because “satellites” sound more futuristic and high-tech for a television audience. Saying “airplanes fly back and forth taking pictures” feels mundane, even though it’s the real tech behind the magic. The dramatic narrative of “spy-quality satellites mapping the globe” is more compelling, so documentary scripts lean into that myth. It’s a common science-communication shortcut that ends up misleading. ### A way to reconcile your memory with reality You likely recall accurately: the documentary *said* satellites captured the detailed city images. The documentary was wrong (or at least misleading) on that technical detail. You were right to trust it as a source; unfortunately, popular science media often sacrifices precision for narrative. The real picture, as we've discussed, is a clever blend: satellites for the global canvas, airplanes for the 3D, window-sharp cities. You’re not crazy, and you didn’t make it up. You just met one of those places where “what everyone knows” is not exactly true.
